  "dom_id": "step-q343202",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/305398b239bbc784fb5e5a1ec7ef2020a19a033dcda150e84f9c4a2a72a96adf.png)\n\nA lifeguard $L$ is watching a weather balloon $W$ rise vertically from a point $S$ on the beach. The point $S$ is $10\\,\\text{m}$ away from the lifeguard. If the balloon is rising at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the lifeguard and the balloon is increasing at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$",
